    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The western sectors held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the United Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> were merged on 23 May 1949 into the Federal Republic, which chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> as a provisional capital to stress that two German states were meant to be temporary.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-plan">Marshall Plan</a> aid and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wirtschaftswunder">Wirtschaftswunder</a> from the early 1950s, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> membership in 1955 and a founding place in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>'s course; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarland">Saarland</a> joined on 1 January 1957.</li><li>Reunification on 3 October 1990 brought the five re-established eastern states into the Federal Republic, which regained full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> on 15 March 1991 and is now the world's third-largest exporter and importer.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> has the world's oldest universal health care system, dating from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Bismarck</a>'s social legislation of the 1880s; the Federal Republic still insures its population by statute, and spent 12.27% of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> on health in 2024.</p></aside>
    </section>

