    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer.jpg" alt="Konrad Adenauer" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer">Konrad Adenauer</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_B_145_Bild-F078072-0004,_Konrad_Adenauer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Katherine Young (1906–1993), CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Federal Republic was built on the ruins of Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. After the surrender of 8 May 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> abolished the German state in law and divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and the remaining territory into four occupation zones; around 12 million ethnic Germans had been expelled from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, and Germany had lost roughly a quarter of its pre-war territory. Surviving Nazi officials were tried at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> trials. On 23 May 1949 the western sectors, controlled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the United Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, were merged into the Federal Republic of Germany; on 7 October 1949 the Soviet Zone became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a>. The new state chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> as a provisional capital to emphasise that the two-state solution was temporary.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> was established as a federal parliamentary republic with a social market economy under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basic-law-for-the-federal-republic-of-germany">Grundgesetz</a>, the Basic Law of 1949. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer">Konrad Adenauer</a> was elected the first federal chancellor that year. The country had already become a major recipient of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-plan">Marshall Plan</a> aid in 1948, and prolonged growth, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wirtschaftswunder">Wirtschaftswunder</a>, began in the early 1950s. Its land ran from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and the Baltic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/states-of-germany">Länder</a> with their own constitutions. Today the republic covers 357,022 square kilometres and has 84.7 million people by the 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, the most populous member state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>; Berlin is the largest city and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> the largest urban area.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wirtschaftswunder"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wirtschaftswunder.jpg" alt="Wirtschaftswunder" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wirtschaftswunder">Wirtschaftswunder</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1000000th_Beetle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mewtu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The social market economy of 1949 produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wirtschaftswunder">Wirtschaftswunder</a> and, after reunification, the largest economy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by nominal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> and the world's third-largest, sixth by purchasing power. Services make up about 72% of GDP, industry 27% (the largest manufacturing output in Europe) and agriculture 1%. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> is the world's third-largest exporter and importer, with the second-largest trade surplus after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; its main exports are vehicles, machinery and chemicals. The automotive industry, with Volkswagen Group the world's second-largest producer, is the most competitive of its sectors; 29 of the Fortune Global 500 firms of 2024 were German, and the Mittelstand of specialised small and medium firms holds around 48% of the world's hidden champions.</p><p>Germany introduced the euro in 2002 and accounted for 28% of the eurozone economy in 2017. Research is a pillar of the economy, with the Max Planck Society, the Helmholtz Association and the Fraunhofer Society, and the dual apprenticeship system is copied around the world. The energy transition, the Energiewende, phased out the last nuclear plants in 2023 and met 40% of power demand from renewables in 2018. Since the 1970s deaths have exceeded births; the population is the world's fourth oldest, with an average age of 47.8, and grows by immigration: 23.8 million people, 28.7%, had a migration background in 2022. Christians were 49.7% in the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and Muslims an estimated 5.3–5.6 million.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berlin-wall.jpg" alt="Berlin Wall" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berlinermauer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Noir, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Federal Republic's armed forces, the Bundeswehr, are organised into the Heer with the KSK special forces, the Marine, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> and the Cyber and Information Domain Service. The constitution describes their role as defensive only, but a ruling of the Federal Constitutional Court in 1994 widened defence to crisis reaction and conflict prevention anywhere in the world. In peacetime the Minister of Defence commands; in a state of defence the Chancellor would become commander-in-chief. Military service was compulsory for men at eighteen until 2011, when it was suspended for a voluntary service, and women have served in all functions since 2001. In May 2024 the Bundeswehr had 180,215 active soldiers and 80,761 civilians.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1955, faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a> from 1961 and eased tensions with the East through Willy Brandt's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostpolitik">Ostpolitik</a> in the late 1960s. After reunification <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> sent peacekeepers to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> with NATO. Spending stayed below the alliance's 2% target, at $73.1 billion or 1.64% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> in 2023, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 2022, when Chancellor Olaf Scholz announced a one-time infusion of 100 billion euros, almost double the 53 billion euro budget of 2021; in 2026 Germany reported $147 billion to NATO, 2.69% of GDP, and its spending in 2025 was the world's fourth-highest in absolute terms. About 3,000 troops serve abroad, 1,800 of them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and its neighbours.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bundesrat-germany"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bundesrat-germany.jpg" alt="Bundesrat (Germany)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bundesrat-germany">Bundesrat (Germany)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesrat_Logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bundesrepublik Deutschland, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> is a federal, parliamentary, representative democratic republic under the Basic Law of 1949. Legislative power rests with the Bundestag, elected by personalised proportional representation with a threshold of 5% or three seats, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundesrat-germany">Bundesrat</a>, whose members are appointed by the governments of the sixteen federated states. Amendments need two-thirds of both houses, and the principles of human dignity, the separation of powers, the federal structure and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a> are valid in perpetuity. The president, Frank-Walter Steinmeier since 2017, is head of state with mainly representative powers and is chosen by the Bundesversammlung of Bundestag members and state delegates; the chancellor, head of government, is appointed by the president after election by the party or coalition with most seats, Friedrich Merz since 2025.</p><p>Since 1949 the party system has been dominated by the centre-right Christian Democratic Union and the centre-left Social Democratic Party, which have supplied every chancellor, with the Free Democrats and Alliance 90/The Greens as junior partners; The Left has sat in the Bundestag since 2007 and the far-right Alternative for Germany since 2017. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> system rests on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>, with the Federal Constitutional Court holding judicial review, specialised supreme courts for labour, social, fiscal and administrative matters, and lay judges, the Schöffen, sitting with professionals on most criminal benches.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/maastricht-treaty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maastricht-treaty.jpg" alt="Maastricht Treaty" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/maastricht-treaty">Maastricht Treaty</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GER_%E2%80%94_BY_%E2%80%94_Regensburg_-_Donaumarkt_1_(Museum_der_Bayerischen_Geschichte;_Vertrag_von_Maastricht)_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mateus2019, CC BY 2.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Federal Republic became the anchor of European integration. A founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>, it signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht-treaty">Maastricht Treaty</a> in 1992 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> Treaty in 2007 and co-founded the eurozone; the European Central Bank sits in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, its largest financial centre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> maintains 227 diplomatic missions and relations with more than 190 countries, belongs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oecd">OECD</a>, the G7, the G20, the World Bank and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">IMF</a>, has kept a strong alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and all its neighbours since 1990 and is widely considered a great power. Its governments are close political allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and it was the world's second-biggest aid donor in 2019.</p><p>Reunification made 3 October the national day, celebrated as German Unity Day since 1990. The country has the third-highest number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>, 55, and is a major destination for immigrants, second only to the United States; it took in over a million refugees and migrants during the 2015 crisis and recorded 1.06 million refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> by April 2023. German is the most widely spoken first language in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>. The Frankfurt Book Fair is the most important in the world for international deals, German films won the foreign-language Oscar in 1979, 2002 and 2007, the Berlinale has run since 1951, Kraftwerk and Tangerine Dream pioneered electronic music, and the national football team won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">World Cup</a> in 1954, 1974, 1990 and 2014.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Reunification set the republic's tasks for a generation. United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was treated as the enlarged continuation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> and kept its memberships; by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>/<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> Act of 1994 Berlin became the capital again while Bonn kept some ministries as a federal city, and the move of the government was completed in 1999. Modernisation of the East German economy was scheduled to last until 2019. The government approved a 50 billion euro stimulus in 2009, and the main projects of the early twenty-first century have been European integration, the Energiewende, the debt brake for balanced budgets, pronatalist measures and Industry 4.0. In the 2005 elections Angela Merkel became the first female chancellor.</p><p>The present decade has brought new strains. The far-right Alternative for Germany has sat in the Bundestag since 2017, and the fertility rate of 1.36 is far below replacement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 2022 brought over a million refugees and a rearmament that carried defence spending past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>'s 2% target by 2026, with about 3,000 troops abroad. The last nuclear plants closed in 2023, when Germany was still the 14th-highest emitter of greenhouse gases, and violent crime reached a fifteen-year high in 2024. Church membership has fallen: a 2025 study estimated 48.1% of the population belonged to no religious organisation. Under Chancellor Friedrich Merz since 2025 the Federal Republic remains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s largest economy and a pillar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was formed on 23 May 1949 from the three western occupation zones, grew through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wirtschaftswunder">Wirtschaftswunder</a> into a founding member of the EEC and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, absorbed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a> on 3 October 1990 and regained full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in 1991. Today it is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s largest economy, the world's third-largest exporter and a great power inside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>.</em></p>

